</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Remarkably, the two notions of phase transitions zero-freeness and correlation decay show some inherent connections.
In seminal works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DS85</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DS87</a>]</cite>, Dobrushin and Shlosman proved that the correlation decay property is actually equivalent to the analyticity of the free energy
and zero-freeness of the partition function for the special case of lattices.
Recently, some more refined connections between them have been observed in the hard-core model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR19</a>]</cite>, the Ising model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS22</a>]</cite>, and the general 2-spin system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite> on graphs of bounded degree.
In these results, one can extend real parameters <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> in which correlation decay exists to their complex neighborhoods where the partition function is zero-free.
However, it is not known whether correlation decay or SSM directly implies zero-freeness of the partition function.
On the other hand, Gamarnik <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gam23</a>]</cite> showed that zero-freeness of the partition function
directly implies a weak form of SSM for the hardcore model and other
graph homomorphism models on graphs with certain properties.
Later, Regts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite> showed that zero-freeness in fact implies SSM for the hardcore model and other
graph homomorphism models on all graphs of bounded degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we further study the direction from zero-freeness of the partition function to obtain SSM for the general 2-spin system.
We show that zero-freeness of the partition function implies SSM for 2-spin systems on arbitrary graphs.
Unlike the hard-core model where the partition function is a univariate function on <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and the zero-free region of <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is in a strip shape, in this paper we view the partition function <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system, as a <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate</em> function over three <em id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex</em> parameters <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we allow the zero-free regions of <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to be of arbitrary shapes as long as they contain some particular points.
For such zero-free regions, we are able to show SSM holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we formally describe our result.
A region refers to a standard complex region i.e., a simply connected open set in <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a neighborhood of some <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a standard complex neighborhood, i.e., a region containing <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (complex) quasi-neighborhood of <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> if either <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a neighborhood of <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\{z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{z\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a region <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set <math id="S1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math> which is still a region.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be one of the following cases:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are not both zeros, and
</span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denote <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 5 (Feasible partial configuration). ‣ 2.1 Notations and definitions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, for the Ising model, we have the following SSM result from zero-free regions of <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a quasi-neighborhood of some <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\backslash\{0\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\backslash\{0\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Ising model is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding Ising model exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Roughly speaking, the first condition of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> requires that the partition function viewed as a function on <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a complex neighborhood of <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second and third conditions of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> together require that the partition function viewed as a function on <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a neighborhood of <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although we give three different conditions for the 2-spin system and a condition for the Ising model under which zero-freeness implies SSM, they are all proved by a unified approach in which a Christoffel-Darboux type identity plays a key role.
A Christoffel-Darboux type identity was originally presented for the independence polynomial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gut91</a>]</cite> on trees, and we extend it to the 2-spin system on trees.
Such an identity gives a relation for the partition functions of the 2-spin system with different pinned vertices, and might be of independent interests.
Our approach uses certain results from complex analysis such as normal functions and Montel theorem which were originally used in Regts’ result for the hard-core model.
In addition, we initially use Riemann mapping theorem as a powerful tool to deal with zero-free regions of arbitrary shapes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The zero-freeness conditions in
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are general enough to cover all currently known zero-free regions (to our best knowledge) of the partition function of the 2-spin system with pinned vertices (the results in the first 5 rows and the last row in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Thus,
our result gives a generic approach to obtain SSM from zero-freeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the zero-freeness results in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where pinned vertices are allowed, SSM holds for the 2-spin system under those settings no matter whether the graph degree is bounded or not.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">For the celebrated Lee-Yang circle theorem which gives a zero-freeness result for the Ising model on graphs where no pinned vertices are allowed, we give a corollary which extends the zero-freeness result to graphs with pinned vertices.
Then by applying Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and its extension to the Ising model with non-uniform external fields (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem34" title="Definition 34. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), we get the following SSM result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ferromagnetic Ising model on graphs of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> specified by <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and a <em id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">complex</em> external field <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM if <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ferromagnetic Ising model on graphs of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> specified by <math id="Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform <em id="Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">positive</em> external fields <math id="Thmtheorem4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛌</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM if <math id="Thmtheorem4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By Weitz’s algorithm, there is an FPTAS for computing the partition function of such a ferromagnetic Ising model.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">For the ferromagnetic Ising model, a <em id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fully polynomial-time randomized approximation scheme</em> (FPRAS) based on the Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) method for all graphs, all positive <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and all positive non-uniform external fields <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> except for the value <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math> on the Lee-Yang circle has been long known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">JS93</a>]</cite>.
Recently, based on the zero-freeness method, an FPTAS was obtained for the ferromagnetic Ising model with a uniform <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on graphs of bounded degree except for the Lee-Yang circle <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19b</a>]</cite>.
On the other hand, #P-hardness was showed for the arc of the circle <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=1</annotation></semantics></math> where Lee-Yang zeros are dense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BGPR22</a>]</cite>.
Our Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> makes a step towards an FPTAS for the ferromagnetic Ising model using the correlation decay method.
More than 70 years after the Lee-Yang circle theorem was proposed, the three main methods MCMC, correlation decay and zero-freeness to devise approximation counting algorithms start tending to meet in the ferromagnetic Ising model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we give some definitions and notations, and we introduce certain results from complex analysis.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we propose our approach and prove a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the 2-spin system on trees. We apply the approach to show that zero-freeness implies SSM for the external field <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we apply the same approach to show that zero-freeness implies SSM for <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we extend our main result to the multivariate setting and apply it to obtain a new SSM result for the ferromagnetic Ising model with non-uniform external fields from the Lee-Yang circle theorem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Notations and definitions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following notations to denote the partition functions of the 2-spin system conditioning on certain partial configurations.
For <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V(G)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V(G),</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
