<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The above proof of LDC for the hard-core model using the Christoffel-Darboux type identity may somewhat answer the following interesting question: <em id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">if the partition of the hard-core model is zero-free on a complex neighborhood of some point <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, can we get SSM for this zero-free region?</em>
The answer would be true if the LDC property could hold for rational functions <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}^{v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}^{v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> expanded as Taylor series near <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the Christoffel-Darboux type identity implies that such a LDC property does not hold. Otherwise, for any graph <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and any vertex <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we would have <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}(\lambda)-P^{u^{+}}_{G,v}(\lambda)=(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}H(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}(\lambda)-P^{u^{+}}_{G,v}(\lambda)=(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}H(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for some function <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can easily check that this is false.
Thus, it is highly possible that a zero-free region not containing <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> cannot imply SSM (at least not by our approach).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Now, we extend the above Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the hard-core model on trees to the general 2-spin system on trees.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 22</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a partial configuration on some <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> which maybe empty, and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are two distinct vertices in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\backslash\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\backslash\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unique path in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(p_{uv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(p_{uv})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of points in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[p_{uv}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[p_{uv}]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of neighbors of points in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[p_{uv}]\setminus V(p_{uv})=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[p_{uv}]\setminus V(p_{uv})=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the component of <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\setminus p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\setminus p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for the partition function of the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning on <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have (we omit the argument <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> in the second line for simplicity)</span></p>
