<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">One can also define point-to-point LDC for </span><math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> near </span><math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> similar to the definition of point-to-point LDC for </span><math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> near </span><math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (Definition </span><a href="#Thmtheorem18" title="Definition 18 (Point-to-point LDC). ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a><span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). Similarly, one can prove the following lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 28</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If a 2-spin system satisfies point-to-point LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, then it also satisfies LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 29</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix complex parameters <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of graphs and <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex neighbourhood of <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the corresponding 2-spin system satisfies LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It suffices to prove that the system satisfies point-to-point LDC for <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the SAW tree construction and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we only need to consider the case that <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree.
Then by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem22" title="Theorem 22. ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>, we have</span></p>
