Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Combine Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 29. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem30" title="Lemma 30 (Uniform bound on a circle centered at 1/𝛾). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> with Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> and Riemann mapping theorem.
The proof is similar to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 26. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Symmetrically, we can show that zero-freeness implies SSM for </span><math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 32</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SSM for <math id="Thmtheorem32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem32.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> respectively where <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∉</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In addition, for the Ising model, since </span><math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> implies </span><math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Thus, we can show that zero-freeness implies SSM for </span><math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> around </span><math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 33</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SSM for <math id="Thmtheorem33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in the Ising model)<span id="Thmtheorem33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a quasi-neighborhood of some <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\backslash\{0\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\backslash\{0\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding Ising model exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In this section,
we apply Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 26. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a><span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to the ferromagnetic Ising model, and get new SSM property for it based on the
celebrated Lee-Yang circle theorem </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">LY52</a><span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Note that Lee-Yang circle theorem gives zero-freeness for the partition function of the Ising model regarding the external field </span><math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Thus, we need to apply Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 26. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a><span id="S5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, instead of Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem33" title="Theorem 33 (SSM for 𝛽 in the Ising model). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a><span id="S5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> which requires zero-freeness regarding the edge activity</span><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          Zeros of the partition function of the Ising model regarding <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are known as Fisher zeros <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fis65</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span><span id="S5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Furthermore, we extend the SSM result to the ferromagnetic Ising model
with non-uniform external fields.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 34</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A 2-spin system defined on a graph <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to has non-uniform external fields if vertices <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> may have different external fields <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The partition function of such a system is defined to be</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda}):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda}):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem34.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem34.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(\sigma)=\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\prod\limits_{v\in V,\sigma(v)=+}\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><munder><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma)=\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\prod\limits_{v\in V,\sigma(v)=+}\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The notations and definitions for the 2-spin system with a uniform external field can be extended to the setting with non-uniform external fields as above. In particular, the Christoffel-Darboux type identity (Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem22" title="Theorem 22. ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a><span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) is also valid for the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields by replacing </span><math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with </span><math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> where </span><math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the unique path connecting </span><math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in a tree.
The SAW tree construction also works where all vertices in </span><math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">SAW</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> mapped from a single vertex </span><math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> have the same external field as the external field </span><math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> of the original </span><math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and
Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a><span id="S5.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> still holds for the multivariate rational functions </span><math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 35</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lee-Yang circle theorem)<span id="Thmtheorem35.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math>
For any graph <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛌</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the ferromagnetic Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform external fields <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛌</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero if for all <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Recall that our Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 26. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a><span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> requires the zero-freeness property holds for the partition function of the Ising model on graphs even conditioning on some partial configurations.
Thus, Lee-Yang circle theorem cannot be directly adapted to our setting.
In order to apply our theorem to derive SSM from zero-freeness, we first give the following corollary of Lee-Yang circle theorem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 36</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any graph <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">E</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛌</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the ferromagnetic Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform external fields <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛌</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero if for all <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
