<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for some functions <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same form of equation holds for <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we can define point-to-point LDC and LDC for functions <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and show that point-to-point LDC implies LDC.
Thus, the functions <math id="S5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy LDC.
Combine the uniform bound on a circle and LDC, and we get SSM by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 16. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
