<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finally, we can turn the Lee-Yang zero-free region to the SSM property for the ferromagnetic Ising model with non-uniform external fields.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 39</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ferromagnetic Ising model on graphs <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> specified by <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform positive external fields <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM if <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">inf</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">sup</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">By spin reversal symmetry (Remark <a href="#Thmremark6" title="Remark 6. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), it suffices to consider the case <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">sup</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, zero-freeness is guaranteed by Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem36" title="Corollary 36. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>, and then SSM follows by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem38" title="Lemma 38. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Concluding remarks</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We prove that if the partition function of the 2-spin system is zero-free in a complex neighbourhood of </span><math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> containing a positive point, then the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.
By a Christoffel-Darboux type identity, it seems like that the implication from zero-free regions to SSM only works for complex neighbourhoods of </span><math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
A natural question is </span><em id="S6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">what is the magic of these two special settings of parameters <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> for which the exact computation of the partition function is polynomial-time solvable?</em><span id="S6.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
In fact, so far all currently known zero-free regions (and even all SSM results and all FPTAS results) for the 2-spin system lie in some complex neighborhoods of </span><math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (up to a switch of the spins </span><math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) or </span><math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
We are curious whether this is always true and we make the following conjectures.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that are not both zeros.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Ising model is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Z</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">U</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For the Ising model, a complex zero-free neighborhood of </span><math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is known </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19a</a><span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Also, one can check that the point </span><math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (with a fixed </span><math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) satisfies the real contraction property introduced in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S6.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a><span id="S6.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S6.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> which implies that there exists a complex neighborhood of </span><math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that the partition function of the Ising model is zero-free on it.
Thus, for Conjecture </span><a href="#Thmconjecture3" title="Conjecture 3. ‣ 6 Concluding remarks ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S6.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, at least there exist complex neighborhoods of </span><math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> respectively such that the partition function of the Ising model is zero-free on them.</span></p>
